questions.prompt:
Binomial or not? For each situation below, decide whether the random variable X has a binomial distribution. If it does, state n and p. If it does not, name the requirement that fails.

Reminder — a binomial experiment must have ALL FOUR of these:
(1) a fixed number of trials, n, decided before you start;
(2) independent trials (the result of one trial does not change the chances on another);
(3) only two outcomes on each trial ("success" = the thing being counted, "failure" = anything else);
(4) the same probability of success, p, on every trial.
X must count the number of successes in the n trials.
